# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Because the platform is built domain-first, the words the courts use are the words the
code uses. Learning them early saves more time than learning any framework here, and
they are the terms to search for when you are looking for where something happens.

These are the terms most likely to come up in your first week. It is a starting point,
not a complete glossary.

## A case, and the people and offences in it

A **prosecution case** is what a prosecuting authority sends to the court. It is the
container everything else hangs from.

A **defendant** is a person or organisation the case is brought against. A case can
have more than one, and much of the platform's complexity comes from handling several
defendants whose circumstances differ.

An **offence** is what is alleged. A **charge** is the formal accusation of an offence
against a particular defendant. The distinction matters: offences are defined
centrally, and a charge is one use of a definition against one person.

## Hearings, and what comes out of them

A **hearing** is an occasion on which the court considers a case. Cases are **listed**
into hearings, which is its own discipline — matching cases to courtrooms, dates and
the people required.

**Results** are what the court decided. This is the centre of gravity of the whole
system: a result is the outcome recorded against a charge or a defendant, and almost
everything downstream is driven by it. Results are structured rather than free text,
which is what makes them actionable.

**Result definitions** are the catalogue of outcomes a court can record, with the
prompts each one asks for. They are reference data, maintained centrally, and they
change over time — which means a question about results usually has a date in it.

## Notices, orders and warrants

**NOWs** — notices, orders and warrants — are the documents a court produces after a
hearing. They are the platform's most visible output, because they are what a person
actually receives. Examples give the best sense of the range:

- Notice of Grant of Bail
- Notice of Financial Penalty
- Notice of Disqualification from Driving
- Notice of New Date of Hearing
- Notice of Football Banning Order
- Notice of Compensation

A single hearing can generate several, addressed to different recipients, and which
ones are produced follows from the results recorded. So NOWs are the clearest
illustration of why results are structured: a document is generated from a decision,
not typed up afterwards.

## Reference data

A good deal of the platform's behaviour is not in code but in **reference data** — the
offence definitions, result definitions, prompts, court and prosecutor details.

Two things follow. Behaviour can change without a release, because the data changed.
And a question of the form "why did it do that" is often answered by the reference data
in force at the time, rather than by the code as it stands now.

## Why the vocabulary is worth the effort

Domain-first design means these words are load-bearing. If you search for the business
term, you will usually find the code; if you search for a technical synonym you have
invented, you often will not. When a term seems to be used in two different ways, that
is worth pursuing rather than smoothing over — it usually marks a real boundary
between two parts of the business.
